Subject: [PATCH] A window lights in its own shape and its drawn shaft goes

Patch 0034 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The user's test of 0032 in the Temple of Time, 2026-10-08. The windows lit the hall, but "they
don't include their top points at the very top of the window ... They're just a flat rectangular
object that hits the floor"; and looking up at the tall window over the pedestal, where the
game's own drawn shaft leaves the glass in the glass's shape: "that's exactly kind of what I want.
The main lights in the main room to do as well". And in the Master Sword room the drawn shaft
lies on the floor as a hard bright patch that never matches where the traced light lands: "only
hide that beam and its floor brightness element when ray tracing is enabled and we're using
proper ray tracing".

This temple's windows are pointed: straight sides up to a shoulder, then two edges meeting at a
point at the top. The window finder now takes the shoulder, the highest corner at the full width,
and the light and the glass test use the open half width at each height, narrowing above the
shoulder to nothing at the top. A rectangular window keeps its shoulder at the top.

A drawn shaft falling the way the shaft traced to a window fell is drawn as nothing while the
local lights are traced (level 2 and above, Light strength above nothing, rung 37 not chosen).
The window's traced light stands in for it. A shaft falling any other way, a song's upright
cylinder, is drawn as before.

diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -536,6 +536,23 @@ namespace RT64 {
